# ─── Core ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ─── Data layer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local development runs Postgres in Docker (`npm run infra:up`). Host port
# 5433 avoids clashing with a locally installed Postgres.
#
# Deployments use Supabase. It hands out two connection strings and they are
# not interchangeable:
#
#   DATABASE_URL         the pooler (Supavisor), port 6543, `?pgbouncer=true`
#                        The app wants this. It opens many short queries, and a
#                        direct connection per machine would exhaust the
#                        project's connection budget.
#
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL  the direct session connection, port 5432
#                        Migrations want this. A transaction-mode pooler may
#                        hand consecutive statements to different backends, and
#                        a migration does not survive that. prisma.config.ts
#                        prefers this automatically when it is set.
#
# The budget guard's lock is `pg_advisory_xact_lock`, which is transaction
# scoped and therefore safe through a transaction-mode pooler. A session-scoped
# `pg_advisory_lock` would not have been — do not "simplify" it to one.
#
# Two things the copied strings do NOT come with, and both fail on connect:
#
#   1. Percent-encode the password. Supabase generates passwords containing
#      @ : / ? # — none survive a URL parser raw. An unencoded @ makes the
#      parser read part of the password as the hostname.
#      Get it with:  node -e 'console.log(encodeURIComponent(process.argv[1]))' '<pw>'
#
#   2. `uselibpqcompat=true` alongside `sslmode=require`. Supabase's pooler
#      presents a certificate from a private CA. libpq reads `require` as
#      "encrypt, do not verify"; node-postgres ≥8.11 reads the same word as
#      verify-full and rejects the chain with "self-signed certificate in
#      certificate chain". The flag is pg's own switch back to libpq
#      semantics — it is not a workaround, it is the documented spelling.
#      For chain verification instead, download the project CA from
#      Settings → Database → SSL Configuration and use
#      `sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/abs/path/prod-ca.crt` with no flag.
#
# Supabase (copy both from Project Settings → Database → Connection string):
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<enc-pw>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&sslmode=require&uselibpqcompat=true
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<enc-pw>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require&uselibpqcompat=true
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://averis:averis@localhost:5433/averis?schema=public
# Leave empty when DATABASE_URL is not a pooler; both then resolve to the same
# connection and nothing about local development changes.
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=
# `prisma migrate dev` creates a scratch database to detect drift, and hosted
# Postgres refuses CREATE DATABASE. Point this at the local Docker instance; it
# never holds real data. Only needed when authoring migrations against Supabase.
SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=
# Real connections one process may hold; defaults to 10, the driver default.
#
# The pooler's budget is shared by the API, every worker and the operator, so
# lowering this is the right move on Supabase — but derive it from process
# count rather than picking a small number. Below this repository's own
# concurrency it does not slow things down, it breaks them: an interactive
# transaction that waits too long for a free connection is rolled back
# underneath itself and reports "Transaction already closed", which points
# nowhere near the pool. Measured: at 5 the integration suite fails about one
# run in three.
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=
# Local Docker (`npm run infra:up`). Deployments need a managed instance, and
# BullMQ constrains which kind: it holds a blocking read open per queue, so an
# HTTP/REST Redis endpoint cannot serve it — the URL must be real TCP, and
# every managed provider serves that only over TLS as `rediss://`.
#
# That same blocking read is what makes a *pay-per-command* free tier the wrong
# shape here. This protocol has four queues, each with a worker whose blocking
# read re-issues roughly every five seconds; idle, that is on the order of a
# million commands a month before a single job is processed. Prefer a tier
# metered by memory:
#
#   Redis Cloud free    30MB, unlimited commands, no sleep  ← fits BullMQ
#   Upstash free        500K commands/month                 ← drains while idle
#
# 30MB is ample: `keepCompleted`/`keepFailed` in packages/queue/src/bullmq.ts
# cap retained history per queue.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# ─── API gateway ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
API_PORT=4000
API_HOST=0.0.0.0
# Comma-separated *root* keys: full access to every tenant's jobs. The workers,
# the operator and the demo run as root. Empty = auth disabled (dev only).
# Per-account keys are minted separately and stored hashed:
#   npm run key:create -- --handle alice
API_KEYS=dev-key-local
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=120
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW=1 minute

# ─── Queue ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# bullmq | memory   (memory lets workers run without Redis, for dev/CI)
QUEUE_DRIVER=bullmq   # pgmq (Postgres, no Redis) | bullmq | memory
QUEUE_PREFIX=averis
JOB_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=4

# ─── Reppo integration ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# http     -> live Reppo Platform API (public read endpoints, no auth required)
# fixture  -> offline recorded fixtures, for tests and air-gapped demos
REPPO_PROVIDER=http
REPPO_API_BASE_URL=https://reppo.ai/api/v1
REPPO_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
REPPO_CACHE_TTL_MS=60000
# Optional. Only needed for authenticated /me/* endpoints (publishing, voting).
# The intelligence layer only reads /public/*, so this is normally left blank.
REPPO_PRIVY_TOKEN=
REPPO_AGENT_API_KEY=

# ─── LLM providers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# mock | anthropic | openai | gemini
LLM_PROVIDER=mock
LLM_MODEL=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=
# Optional unified gateway (routes any "provider/model" string)
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# ─── Web app ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000
AVERIS_API_KEY=dev-key-local

# ─── Operator (autonomous node) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
OPERATOR_CONFIG=./apps/operator/operator.yaml

# ─── Inbound payments (x402, disabled by default) ───────────────────────────
# Puts POST /v1/jobs behind an HTTP 402 paywall. Off unless X402_ENABLED=true,
# and refuses to start when on without a recipient and a facilitator.
X402_ENABLED=false
# A label for logs and the challenge. It identifies nothing on the wire; the
# chain id below is what does.
X402_NETWORK=robinhood
# EVM chain id payments settle on. Required: there is no default, because a
# wrong chain id is not a misconfiguration, it is money sent to another network.
X402_CHAIN_ID=
# RPC endpoint for that chain.
X402_RPC_URL=
# Address payments settle to, as 0x + 40 hex. No default: a default would be
# someone else's.
X402_PAY_TO=
# Service that verifies and settles the payment. Never holds funds.
X402_FACILITATOR_URL=
# Flat access fee per job, in whole units of the asset below. Flat because the
# price is quoted before the request body is parsed — see docs/architecture.md.
X402_PRICE=0.10
# USDC contract on this chain, as 0x + 40 hex. Required, and validated at
# startup: there is no canonical address to fall back to across EVM chains.
X402_ASSET=

# ─── Resolution oracles (both disabled by default) ──────────────────────────
# What a prediction is scored against once its deadline passes. Curation always
# runs — it reads the data network the protocol already talks to. These two
# reach outside it, so each is opt-in: an oracle registered without a way to
# reach its source would turn a missing setting into a run of failed
# resolutions instead of a clean "no oracle supports this source".
#
# A prediction names its oracle in `criteria.source`.
#
# Price. Source `price:<BASE>-<QUOTE>`, e.g. `price:ETH-USD`; metric `spot`.
# Quotes are read from two keyless public venues and refused if they disagree,
# because picking one of two conflicting prices is a coin flip that would land
# in an agent's accuracy score as if it were a measurement.
ORACLE_PRICE_ENABLED=false

# On-chain. Source `chain:<chainId>` or `chain:<chainId>:<address>`; metrics
# `block_number`, `native_balance`, `erc20_total_supply`, `erc20_balance_of:<addr>`.
# One variable per chain, each a comma-separated endpoint list. A chain with no
# variable here is simply unsupported. Note these read `latest`, not the state
# as of the deadline: most public nodes are pruned, so a historical call fails
# rather than answering. The sweep interval is what keeps `latest` close enough
# to the deadline to mean what the prediction asked about.
# ORACLE_RPC_4663=https://rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com
# ORACLE_RPC_8453=https://mainnet.base.org

# How often due predictions are swept, in ms. Also bounds how stale a reading
# can be before the oracles decline it outright.
# RESOLUTION_SWEEP_MS=60000

# ─── Wallet login (Privy, disabled by default) ──────────────────────────────
# When set, an automation is owned by the wallet that deployed it. The browser
# presents a Privy identity token and the *gateway* verifies its signature — a
# wallet address sent as a parameter is a claim anyone can make.
# Both must be set together, or neither; half-configured throws at startup.
# The app id is also read by the web app at runtime (not NEXT_PUBLIC_*, which
# would be baked in at build time).
PRIVY_APP_ID=
PRIVY_APP_SECRET=

# ─── Automation (trading, disabled by default) ──────────────────────────────
# none | paper
#   none  — refuses to open or close anything (the default)
#   paper — books simulated fills at the quoted price and writes nothing on-chain
# There is no live driver. An automation cannot spend real money whatever else
# is set here; see docs/automation.md for why that is absent rather than stubbed.
EXECUTION_DRIVER=none
# Where an exit sweep gets its mark. With none set, nothing opens and no
# position is ever marked — a mark nobody observed would make every number the
# dashboard shows fiction. Point this at a quote endpoint you have verified;
# `{mint}` is substituted into both the URL and the path.
EXECUTION_PRICE_URL=
EXECUTION_PRICE_PATH=data.{mint}.usdPrice
EXECUTION_PRICE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# ─── Settlement (modular, disabled by default in MVP) ───────────────────────
# none | ledger
#   none   — refuses to pay; rewards stay PENDING (the default)
#   ledger — records payments made outside this system so they are not owed twice
# On-chain settlement is not implemented; see docs/protocol.md for why it is
# absent rather than stubbed.
SETTLEMENT_DRIVER=none
# Where the non-agent shares are paid. Agent shares go to the wallet the
# agent's owner registered; an agent without one is skipped, never guessed.
SETTLEMENT_VALIDATOR_ADDRESS=
SETTLEMENT_PROTOCOL_ADDRESS=
SETTLEMENT_TREASURY_ADDRESS=
# The chain an on-chain settlement driver would sign against, when one exists.
# Left blank on purpose: there is no driver yet, and a plausible-looking RPC and
# token address beside code that has never moved a cent is the most misleading
# thing this file could contain.
EVM_RPC_URL=
USDC_TOKEN=

# --- Tracing (optional; records nothing unless set) ---
# Setting the endpoint alone switches the exporter to otlp.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# TRACING_EXPORTER=console
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=averis
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=x-api-key=...
# TRACING_SAMPLE_RATE=1
